### What is the Longest Increasing Subsequence problem?

Given an array of integers, find the length of the longest subsequence where every element is strictly greater than the previous one. Elements do not need to be contiguous. For example, in `[10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18]`, one LIS is `[2, 3, 7, 101]` with length 4.

This is LeetCode 300 and a classic dynamic programming plus binary search problem.

```java
package com.bank.service;

// Stock prices over 8 days: [10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18]
// LIS: [2, 3, 7, 101] or [2, 3, 7, 18] -> length 4
// These are the longest stretches of rising prices (non-contiguous)
```

### How does the O(n squared) DP approach work?

The naive DP defines `dp[i]` as the length of the LIS ending at index i. For each i, check all previous indices j where `nums[j] < nums[i]` and set `dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)`. The answer is `max(dp[i])` over all i.

This works but is O(n squared), which is too slow for large arrays.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LisNaive {
    public int lengthOfLIS(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] dp = new int[n];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(dp, 1);
        int maxLen = 1;

        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (nums[j] < nums[i]) {
                    dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1);
                }
            }
            maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, dp[i]);
        }
        return maxLen;
    }
}
```

### How does the O(n log n) patience sort approach work?

Maintain a list called `tails`. For each number x:
- Binary-search for the leftmost position in tails where `tails[pos] >= x`.
- If no such position exists (x is larger than all tails), append x. This means x extends the longest run.
- Otherwise, replace `tails[pos]` with x. A smaller tail keeps future extension easier.

The tails array is always sorted, which is exactly why binary search is valid. After processing all numbers, `tails.size()` is the LIS length.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class LisFast {

    public int lengthOfLIS(int[] nums) {
        List<Integer> tails = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int x : nums) {
            int pos = Collections.binarySearch(tails, x);
            if (pos < 0) {
                pos = -(pos + 1); // insertion point
            }
            if (pos == tails.size()) {
                tails.add(x); // extends the longest run
            } else {
                tails.set(pos, x); // smaller tail, more extendable
            }
        }
        return tails.size();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] prices = {10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18};
        LisFast solver = new LisFast();
        System.out.println("LIS length: " + solver.lengthOfLIS(prices));
    }
}
```

```text
LIS length: 4
```

### What does the tails array look like during execution?

Walk through `[10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18]`:

1. x=10: tails=[], append -> tails=[10]
2. x=9: pos=0 (9 < 10), replace -> tails=[9]
3. x=2: pos=0 (2 < 9), replace -> tails=[2]
4. x=5: pos=1 (5 > 2), append -> tails=[2, 5]
5. x=3: pos=1 (3 < 5), replace -> tails=[2, 3]
6. x=7: pos=2 (7 > 3), append -> tails=[2, 3, 7]
7. x=101: pos=3, append -> tails=[2, 3, 7, 101]
8. x=18: pos=3 (18 < 101), replace -> tails=[2, 3, 7, 18]

Final tails length = 4. Note: tails=[2, 3, 7, 18] but the actual LIS could be [2, 5, 7, 101]. Tails is not the LIS.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class TailsTracer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] prices = {10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18};
        List<Integer> tails = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int x : prices) {
            int pos = Collections.binarySearch(tails, x);
            if (pos < 0) pos = -(pos + 1);
            if (pos == tails.size()) tails.add(x);
            else tails.set(pos, x);
            System.out.println("x=" + x + " tails=" + tails);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
x=10 tails=[10]
x=9 tails=[9]
x=2 tails=[2]
x=5 tails=[2, 5]
x=3 tails=[2, 3]
x=7 tails=[2, 3, 7]
x=101 tails=[2, 3, 7, 101]
x=18 tails=[2, 3, 7, 18]
```

### Why is tails not the actual LIS?

Tails is a length-tracking device, not a subsequence recorder. The replacements optimize for extendability, not for preserving a real subsequence. In the example above, the final tails are `[2, 3, 7, 18]`, but 18 replaced 101, so the real subsequence that achieved length 4 was `[2, 5, 7, 101]` or `[2, 3, 7, 101]`.

To reconstruct the actual LIS, maintain parent pointers: for each number, record which previous number it extends.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class LisReconstruct {

    public List<Integer> findLIS(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        List<Integer> tails = new ArrayList<>();
        int[] tailIndices = new int[n]; // which index in nums is the tail
        int[] parent = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(parent, -1);
        int[] posToIndex = new int[n]; // tails[pos] came from nums[posToIndex[pos]]

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int pos = Collections.binarySearch(tails, nums[i]);
            if (pos < 0) pos = -(pos + 1);
            if (pos == tails.size()) tails.add(nums[i]);
            else tails.set(pos, nums[i]);
            posToIndex[pos] = i;
            parent[i] = pos > 0 ? posToIndex[pos - 1] : -1;
        }

        // Backtrack from the last element
        List<Integer> lis = new ArrayList<>();
        int idx = posToIndex[tails.size() - 1];
        while (idx != -1) {
            lis.add(nums[idx]);
            idx = parent[idx];
        }
        Collections.reverse(lis);
        return lis;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] prices = {10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18};
        LisReconstruct solver = new LisReconstruct();
        System.out.println("LIS: " + solver.findLIS(prices));
    }
}
```

```text
LIS: [2, 3, 7, 18]
```

### What about strictly increasing vs non-decreasing?

The strictness of the subsequence depends on the binary search bound:
- **Lower bound** (leftmost position where `tails[pos] >= x`): strictly increasing. Equal values get replaced, not appended.
- **Upper bound** (leftmost position where `tails[pos] > x`): non-decreasing. Equal values can be appended.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class StrictnessDemo {
    // For strictly increasing: use lower_bound (the default binarySearch behavior)
    // For non-decreasing: find first position where tails[pos] > x

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {3, 5, 5, 7};
        // Strictly increasing LIS: [3, 5, 7] length 3
        // Non-decreasing LIS: [3, 5, 5, 7] length 4
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

- **Empty array**: return 0.
- **All decreasing**: `[5, 4, 3, 2, 1]` -> LIS length 1 (each element alone).
- **All increasing**: `[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]` -> LIS length 5 (the entire array).
- **All equal**: `[7, 7, 7]` -> strictly increasing LIS length 1.
- **Single element**: LIS length 1.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LisEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LisFast solver = new LisFast();
        System.out.println("Decreasing: " + solver.lengthOfLIS(new int[]{5,4,3,2,1}));
        System.out.println("Increasing: " + solver.lengthOfLIS(new int[]{1,2,3,4,5}));
        System.out.println("All equal:  " + solver.lengthOfLIS(new int[]{7,7,7}));
        System.out.println("Single:     " + solver.lengthOfLIS(new int[]{42}));
    }
}
```

```text
Decreasing: 1
Increasing: 5
All equal:  1
Single:     1
```

### What about the banking use case?

At the National Bank of India, the portfolio analytics desk needs the longest stretch of rising closing prices for a stock to judge momentum. Those rising days do not have to be consecutive. The naive O(n squared) approach is too slow on multi-year daily histories, so the patience-sorting method with binary search gives the answer in O(n log n) time.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class MomentumAnalyzer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] closingPrices = {150, 140, 145, 155, 148, 160, 170, 165, 180};
        LisFast solver = new LisFast();
        int momentum = solver.lengthOfLIS(closingPrices);
        System.out.println("Longest rising streak (non-contiguous): " + momentum + " days");
    }
}
```

```text
Longest rising streak (non-contiguous): 6 days
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What is the time complexity? (O(n log n) because n elements each do one binary search.)
- Is tails the actual LIS? (No, it is a length-tracking device. Reconstruction needs parent pointers.)
- How do you handle non-decreasing instead of strictly increasing? (Use upper bound instead of lower bound.)
- Can you do it in O(n)? (No, the LIS problem has an information-theoretic lower bound of O(n log n).)
- What if you need the actual subsequence? (Maintain parent pointers and backtrack.)
